---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { ADJACENT, PEERS } from "../data/landscape.js";
---

<BaseLayout>
  <header class="site-header">
    <a class="site-wordmark" href="/">meta-harness</a>
    <div class="site-header-end">
      <span class="site-meta">field notes · v0</span>
      <a href="https://github.com/Angelguirao/meta-harness">source</a>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main id="main">
    <header class="hero">
      <h1 class="brand">meta-harness</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Field notes on the layer above coding agents — written from LifeOS,
        before the industry agrees what to call it.
      </p>
    </header>

    <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#pressure">Pressure</a>
      <a href="#above">Above</a>
      <a href="#lifeos">LifeOS</a>
      <a href="#field">Field</a>
      <a href="#parts">Parts</a>
      <a href="#open">Open</a>
    </nav>

    <article>
      <section id="pressure">
        <h2>The pressure</h2>
        <p>
          A harness — Claude Code, Codex, Deep Agents, OpenClaw — is the employee.
          Tools, sandbox, a folder, a session. That job is real. It is also where
          real work breaks:
        </p>
        <p>
          The change spans three repos; the agent sees one. Yesterday’s session
          already ruled out an approach; that reasoning died with the chat. A
          teammate’s agent burned two hours on the same dead end, invisibly.
          Policy lives in <code>AGENTS.md</code> and sometimes the model obeys.
        </p>
        <p>
          Git keeps the diff. The session kept the why. Neither is available to
          the next agent as an organizational fact.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="above">
        <h2>Something sits above</h2>
        <p>
          Meta-harness, control plane, company OS, synthetic monorepo — the
          labels fight. The jobs are clearer:
        </p>
        <ul class="jobs">
          <li>
            <strong>Continuity</strong> — sessions and people build on prior
            attempts, not cold starts.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Scope</strong> — work crosses repos and systems of record
            without you as the plumbing.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Constraint</strong> — spend, writes, and risk are enforced in
            code, not hoped for in prompts.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Meaning</strong> — optionally a world model: durable facts
            that are not chat transcript.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Thin wraps optimize the coding agent. Company OSes hire and budget a
          team of them. Both answer the same pressure. We will not collapse them
          into one slogan until shipping forces the choice.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="lifeos">
        <h2>What LifeOS is testing</h2>
        <p>
          <a class="in-body" href="https://github.com/Angelguirao/personal-ai-os"
            >LifeOS</a
          >
          keeps intelligence and meaning outside the harness. Folio and ledger
          hold truth. PersonalOS walks phases. Fly Deep Agents (and sometimes
          Claude or Codex) are muscle. Agents do not own the plan pointer.
        </p>
        <p class="stack" aria-label="LifeOS stack">
          world model — folio · ledger · trackers<br />
          intelligence — PersonalOS · OPEVL<br />
          coordination — Work · Run · Session<br />
          execution — Fly Deep Agents · optional lab CLIs
        </p>
        <p>
          That already looks like a control plane with harness muscle. It only
          partly looks like Polygraph/Omnigent (handoff, cross-repo, sandbox).
          Which gap is the product — and whether one name covers both — is why
          this notebook exists.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="field">
        <h2>In the field</h2>
        <p>
          Annotated bets on slices of the pressure above. Not a feature matrix.
          Not a ranking.
        </p>
        <ul class="peers">
          {
            PEERS.map((p) => (
              <li>
                <div class="peer-name">
                  {p.url ? (
                    <a href={p.url} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
                      {p.name}
                    </a>
                  ) : (
                    <span>{p.name}</span>
                  )}
                  <span class="layer">{p.layer}</span>
                </div>
                <p>{p.note}</p>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="parts">
        <h2>Parts, not one product shape</h2>
        <p>
          A fuller “layer above” is probably a <strong>bundle</strong>: work
          graph, session memory, cross-repo scope, policy/sandbox, retrieval /
          world model, harness swap. Almost nobody ships the whole bundle with
          equal weight. Peers specialize — Polygraph on space and time across
          repos, Omnigent on session + policy, Paperclip on company coordination,
          Stanford on searching the harness itself.
        </p>
        <p>
          So the useful question is rarely “is X a meta-harness?” It is
          <em>which parts does X own, and which center of gravity is the
          product?</em>
        </p>
        <ul class="peers peers-adjacent">
          {
            ADJACENT.map((p) => (
              <li>
                <div class="peer-name">
                  {p.url ? (
                    <a href={p.url} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
                      {p.name}
                    </a>
                  ) : (
                    <span>{p.name}</span>
                  )}
                  <span class="layer">{p.layer}</span>
                </div>
                <p>{p.note}</p>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
        <p>
          Cerebras Knowledge is a strong bet on the <strong>retrieval /
          distillation</strong> part — meet data where it lives, answer with
          citations, serve humans and agents. That can sit <em>inside</em> a
          meta-harness or cognitive OS (LifeOS already borrows that philosophy).
          It is not, by itself, continuity + scope + constraint around acting
          agents. Same for a pure work tracker or a pure sandbox: necessary
          organs, not the whole animal.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="open">
        <h2>Still open</h2>
        <ol class="questions">
          <li>
            Which parts of the bundle are load-bearing for LifeOS — and which
            can stay thin forever?
          </li>
          <li>
            Is the durable asset the wrap around coding agents, or the world
            model and walker that dispatch them?
          </li>
          <li>
            What must survive a crashed harness for the layer above to have
            earned its keep?
          </li>
          <li>
            For LifeOS: session handoff, policy sandbox, or cross-repo — which
            missing surface forces a placement call first?
          </li>
          <li>
            When, if ever, does this stop being a private OS and become something
            other operators should run?
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>

  <footer class="site-footer">
    <p>
      Beside{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/Angelguirao/personal-ai-os">LifeOS</a>. Peers
      are citations. MIT.
    </p>
  </footer>
</BaseLayout>
